body:
This finishes the parts of #1141 that #1213 did not cover, other than building the registry bundles once.

**Composite actions instead of copy-pasted blocks.** Two new local actions replace the repeated steps. `.github/actions/add-swap` takes a `mode`: `fixed` is the old 10 GB `/swapfile` block (test, test-validate, clippy), and `adaptive` is the block that stops systemd-oomd and sizes swap from the free space on `/` or `/mnt` (doc, rs API compatibility, package, package-workspace). `.github/actions/install-libdbus` is the `apt-get update || true` + `apt-get install libdbus-1-dev pkg-config` step, with its comment, used by seven jobs in rust.yml and the two jobs in publish.yml that had the same block. The shell commands are unchanged, and so are the step names and their positions in each job. rust.yml is path-filtered, so both action directories are added to its `paths:` list. publish.yml keeps its filename, and every job that now uses the action already checks the repository out first.

**Go module cache for `trust-tasks-go/tsp`.** That is the one nested module with third-party dependencies (affinidi-tsp-go and its PQ crypto). Its `setup-go` now has `cache: true` with `cache-dependency-path: trust-tasks-go/tsp/go.sum`. The other nested modules depend only on the published core, so they stay uncached.

**No pub cache, on purpose.** setup-dart has no cache support of its own. An `actions/cache` entry per Dart job and SDK would add roughly twenty entries to a repository cache quota that rust.yml's comments already describe as full enough to evict the Rust caches every run restores. A Rust cache miss costs far more than re-downloading pub packages, so I left the pub cache out. If it is wanted, it should save from `main` only (a split `actions/cache/restore` and `actions/cache/save`), in its own PR.

**Early exits for the hand-written sibling jobs.** The jobs are go.yml `tsp`, `proof`, `didcomm` and `capabilityclient`, dart.yml `packages` (all legs, downgrade included) and ts.yml `packages`. Each now starts with a step that uses the new `.github/actions/changed-paths` action, and every later step is gated on its `run` output. The jobs, their names and the unfiltered workflows are unchanged, so a skipped job still runs and reports success, and it stays usable as a required check. The output is `false` only when all of the following hold:
- the event is `pull_request`;
- the head branch is not `release`;
- the PR's changes could be computed;
- nothing changed under the job's inputs.

The action computes the PR's changes by diffing GitHub's merge commit against its first parent, after fetching one more level of history. Push to main, workflow_dispatch, the Release PR, and any failure to fetch or diff run the full job. Each job's inputs are:
- the package directory;
- its core library: `trust-tasks-go` minus the other nested modules, `trust-tasks-dart`, or `trust-tasks-ts`;
- `fixtures/`, which the proof tests read;
- the job's own workflow file;
- `.github/actions/`, always included.

The Dart downgrade legs read the core's floor from the package's pubspec and resolve against pub.dev, so they are covered by the same inputs. A newly published core is picked up on the next run on main.

**Left for later.** Building the registry bundles once and sharing them as an artifact across rust.yml, ts.yml, go.yml, dart.yml and deploy.yml is not attempted here. Artifacts do not cross workflows without a `workflow_run` chain or a separate reusable workflow, and either would restructure the required-check jobs. ts.yml `gen-equivalence` already restores a rust-cache since #1213, so the remaining cost is a warm `cargo run --release` per workflow. The repeated root `npm ci` is also unchanged.

Verification: `actionlint` is clean on every changed workflow, and `npm run test:checks` and `npm run check` pass. I ran the decision script locally against a real merge commit for these cases:
- pull_request with matching paths gives `true`;
- pull_request whose inputs are untouched gives `false`;
- a `:(exclude)` pathspec works;
- the Release PR gives `true`;
- push gives `true`.

The shallow-fetch path (`git fetch --depth=2` of the merge commit) can only be exercised on Actions, so this PR's own run is its first test. Because this PR changes `.github/actions/`, every sibling job here takes the full path. The skip path will first be seen on the next PR that touches none of a job's inputs.

Part of #1141.

Moves the VTI-ACL-110 row to F.7 Closed.

Closed by:
- trustoverip/dtgwg-cred-spec#84 (VAC `purposes`, context v2) and OpenVTC/dtg-credentials 0.14.0 (the verifier reads it and applies the chain rule);
- OpenVTC/verifiable-trust-infrastructure#2545 (hosts serve `rooms/records/get` 0.4 and hold reads to the chain's purposes);
- OpenVTC/verifiable-trust-infrastructure#2546 (the agent writes a grant's purposes into the credential it issues, `rooms/owner/issue-authority` 0.3).

Merge after #2546. The VTI-ACL-107 row (non-ACL entry-writing paths) stays open.

The badge picture builder offered sixteen icons. It now searches lucide's whole set.

- **Search:** *Find an icon* matches every word of the search against the icon's name (`BadgeCheck` reads "Badge check"). The sixteen offered icons count under their own names, so "tools" finds the wrench. At most 60 show at once, with a count and a hint to narrow the search.
- **Without a search:** the sixteen icons show as before. The chosen icon is named, and stays chosen when the search is cleared.
- **Bundle:** the whole set (1,544 icons) is its own chunk, `badge-icons`, about 104 kB gzipped. It loads only when someone types a search. The main bundle keeps only the sixteen.
- **No wire change:** the picture is still drawn by the browser and sent through `vtc/badges/images/upload` (VTI-BDG-050).
- **Docs:** the operator guide's *Pictures* paragraph.

**Tests:**
- searching "bug", choosing it, and the drawing showing it;
- clearing the search keeps the choice;
- "no match";
- naming;
- multi-word matching;
- the cap.

Checks: `npm run lint` passes, `vitest` passes 1587 of 1587, and both bundles build.

In the first of five full-suite runs, one test failed. The output scrolled past before I could see which test it was. The four runs after it all passed, one of them on a cold cache.
